merge(e9,e10): the run lifecycle and the account identity, coexisting

Two epics that branched from the same commit and wrote to the same thirteen
files. Four needed a hand, and in every one of them the answer was to make
both features present rather than to pick a side.

cmd/innsegl/cli.go registers `retire` (#257) and `admin-credential` (#264), so
the list TestSubcommandSetIsExactlyTheDocumentedFive compares against is the
union of both. Either side alone fails that test against the other side's
cli.go, which is the test doing its job.

scripts/innsegl-commit.sh is the one that mattered. E9 factored the
three-rung registration ladder into try_register; E10 replaced that helper
with inline calls so it could tell exit 3 -- the listener refused the
credential -- from a transport failure that means the deployment is not there.
The exit-3 handling now lives INSIDE the helper, once, and it ENDS the ladder
rather than falling to the next rung: every rung presents the same credential
to the same listener, so a refusal is not something dropping the parent or
dropping repo and branch can satisfy. Answering it three times would print the
schema-1 remedy for a problem that is not the schema, and send an operator to
restart a deployment that is up and answering.

Both self-test suites are append-vs-append in one place. Both blocks are kept
whole, E9's first, with E10's credential section last so its OPS-073 claim --
that everything above it ran against a listener demanding nothing -- still
covers the whole suite rather than part of it. In the signer suite the
fixtures are put back between the two blocks: E9's last case leaves a session
pointer on disk and a register_agent scripted to refuse, and a credential case
that failed for THAT reason would read as a credential fault.

Tests that drove this, both run on the merged tree:

  scripts/hooks/shim-selftest.sh      56 passed, 0 failed
  scripts/innsegl-commit-selftest.sh  28 ok, 0 failed

E9 left those suites at 43 and 18, E10 at 37 and 23, and the merge base at 24
and 13. Every case either epic authored is present and passing. Three cases
inherited from the base are gone, each superseded by the epic that rewrote the
behaviour it asserted: E9 moved parent resolution into the MCP, so the shim no
longer resolves a run id of its own, and E10 narrowed "the shim derives no
workspace" to "no worktree and no branch" because a repository-scoped
credential has to name a repository before the call it authorises.

The lifecycle pins pass unchanged: TestSPI014SecondLapseRecordsASecondWithdrawal,
TestREC017ARestoredRunRaisesNoDriftAlert and
TestMCP078HorizonIsMeasuredFromTheLapseBeingRestored.

`go build`, `go vet` and `golangci-lint run` are clean, and `go test -race
-count=1 -p 1` passes every package but test/smoke, which was not run against
the machine it needs: TestOPS004FreshCloneBootstrap demands an empty Docker
host and refused on a container name a stack already running here holds. It
never reached any merged code. test/deploy, which exercises the same compose
files, passes.

NOT FIXED HERE. `innsegl retire` (#257) is a THIRD caller of the
identity-lifecycle listener and presents no credential. #266 surveyed the
callers and fixed both it could see; `retire` did not exist on that branch.
Measured on this tree, occurrences of `Authorization`: the harness shim 2, the
signer 2, cmd/innsegl/retire.go 0. Wherever the lifecycle listener is served
separately the credential is not optional -- serve refuses to start that
listener without a key set -- so `innsegl retire` is refused at the transport
there, before any tool runs. A deployment serving all tools on one listener is
unaffected. This wants its own issue, a test first, and neither belongs in a
merge.

